## Step 1 — PnP Pose Extraction:

For each image, AprilGrid detector finds tags and extracts corner pixel locations
Frames with fewer than minTagsForValidObs tags are rejected
Detected corners are back-projected from pixels to normalized coordinates using Kalibr's distortion model (removing intrinsics and distortion)
Normalized 2D points + known 3D target corners are passed to OpenCV's solvePnP with identity K and zero distortion
solvePnP returns T_camera_target, Kalibr inverts to get T_t_c
Corner outliers filtered by reprojection error (sigma threshold + absolute floor)
Output: target0Observations and target1Observations — lists of (timestamp, PnP pose, 2D-3D correspondences)
~1090 valid detections out of ~1300 images for cam0

## Step 2 — B-Spline Construction (initPoseSpline):

PnP poses inverted from T_t_c to T_c_t (camera pose in target frame)
4×4 transformation matrices converted to 6-vectors [tx, ty, tz, rx, ry, rz] using axis-angle representation
Time range padded by timeOffsetPadding * 2 on each end (40ms for main spline, 0 for throwaway splines)
Rotation vectors unwrapped to prevent discontinuities — handles both θ + 2πk wrapping and (-axis, -angle) sign ambiguity
B-spline fitted through the discrete poses using initPoseSplineSparse with specified knot density
Output: continuous BSplinePose object queryable at any timestamp within its domain for smooth 4×4 transformation
Spline tracks discrete poses well where data exists, oscillates freely in gaps with no detections

## Step 3 — findTimeshiftPrior summary:

Purpose: estimate a rough time offset between cam0 and cam1 before the main optimization
Builds two independent splines — one from cam0 poses at cam0 timestamps, one from cam1 poses at cam1 timestamps, no padding
The signal used is angular velocity norm — how fast each camera is rotating at each moment, a scalar value over time
Angular velocity norm is frame-independent — both cameras experience the same rotation rate regardless of their orientation
Cross-correlation slides one signal past the other to find the discrete shift that maximizes similarity
Discrete shift × dT (uniform sample spacing) = time offset in seconds

